{"kind":"assert","text":"I7 tuple-reduction breakdown, separate from the adopted group-value traversal amendment: a currently admitted pure JSONata program has two results because reduceTupleStream ranges over tuple fields and appends into shared backing arrays. The exact current [confinement](jsonataddl/confine.go@3a593721231326d589541113add606b270490e35:35) accepts both lexical and AST checks; the original pinned evaluator github.com/jsonata-go/jsonata v0.0.0-20250709164031-599f35f32e5f runs it through its public Evaluate API. No private evaluator replacement is used for that proof.\n\nProgram: ($w := [[0,1,2]]; $rows := [{\"a\":$w,\"b\":$w},{\"a\":[\"A\"],\"b\":[\"B\"]}]; $rows.a@$a.b@$b{\"one\":{\"a\":$a,\"b\":$b}})\n\nTwo independent runs of 1,000 evaluations each produced both {\"one\":{\"a\":[0,1,2,\"A\"],\"b\":[0,1,2,\"A\"]}} and {\"one\":{\"a\":[0,1,2,\"B\"],\"b\":[0,1,2,\"B\"]}}. First run A136/B864; second A148/B852. These are observed outcomes, not probabilities or a production-data claim. The attached pinned-confinement probe and log reproduce the actual compile/AST admission and bounded public evaluations.\n\nThe reducer's first tuple shallow-copies a and b from the same length3/capacity4 slice. Appending A and B while ranging over the second tuple writes the same backing slot; the last field wins for both properties. The unchanged reducer's direct control at capacity3 always preserves separate A and B; capacity4 produces both shared outcomes. Its exact pinned function is attached and byte-identical to the private worktree's legacy reducer (SHA256 1bfe5401977a97648d3c598963913c92193bdb7bf8b3e2d4b9cae931be50d035). This mechanism precedes group-value evaluation; sorting final object serialization or adopting first-encounter group traversal alone does not settle it.\n\nThe current [amendment](notes/2026-09-05-capability-context-contract.md@3a593721231326d589541113add606b270490e35:228) specifies key-discovery and distinct group-value order, while explicitly preserving existing tuple/reduce behavior. Choosing a tuple-field traversal rule or replacing aliasing appends with value-semantic copies needs an explicit compatibility decision, not a silent implementation assumption or blanket tuple refusal. I have not implemented either choice. A follow-up request assigns that authority decision; the full I7 commitment remains live, and independent runtime proof work can continue. No module publication/pin/replace, stored-program reset, installation or live telemetry operation occurred. The temporary probe files were moved out of the two owned worktrees after the runs.","body":{"head":"3a593721231326d589541113add606b270490e35","stale_bases":"git:sha1:da732b0bdaad4426ed4ad666b892d8a7c68f625f#git:sha1:5cfedb59bc1285a0de3609b8dc7ae3221076fa2a stale; retired bases: git:sha1:da732b0bdaad4426ed4ad666b892d8a7c68f625f#git:sha1:8c17d9e03de8cacb255a59d1859dc84ef6f78c70"}}